  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 AM EDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nAnother warm and tranquil day as ridge rebuilds behind departing\nshortwave responsible for current mid/high clouds and virga. \nThese clouds will scatter during the day and highs climb into \nthe mid 70s for most given some light SW flow/WAA. Mild \novernight lows expected given increasing SW gradient and clouds \nahead of the next cold front. Only mid 50s expected in our NW \nzones. As previously mentioned, this cold front will feature a \nmoderate prefrontal theta-e surge (thanks to a 30-40 kt LLJ) \ncapable of finally moistening very dry/stable airmass that has \nbeen in place for quite some time. However, better mid and upper\nlevel dynamics remain displaced well to our north. Decent \n1000-850mb convergence within a moderate theta-e ridge will be \nenough to spark a narrow line of light showers on Thu but total \nrain amounts will be very light (a few hundredths at best). The \nbest chances will be in our northwest where better moisture and \nforcing reside. Areas south of US-24 likely remain dry. No \nthunderstorms expected given virtually no instability so overall\nno impacts anticipated. Postfrontal airmass is cooler but not \nparticularly cold given it is almost Halloween. Lows Thu night \ndip near 40F with highs on Fri in the 60s (still above normal).\n\nForecast details for the weekend and early next week remain \nuncertain. A subtropical Pacific jet streak/shortwave lifts into\nthe region in piecemeal fashion over the weekend followed by \nthe eastward ejection (in some fashion) of a Pacific Northwest \nupper low. This is a convoluted mid/upper level pattern \nevolution that models are still struggling with immensely. The \ninitial shortwave and theta-e surge could bring some light rain \nto our W/NW counties early Sat but CVA and moisture advection \nare both very meager. Any rain will be light and it could easily\nremain entirely dry. These low chance PoP's extend into the \nearly evening. Some light sprinkles can't be entirely ruled out \nfor Sat evening activities but overall impacts should be \nminimal. Better rain chances arrive on Sun but even this is no \nslam dunk. Several guidance sources suggest the main trough and \nbetter moisture pass just to our S/SE. Attention then turns to \nlarge upper low migrating across the country. Models continue to\nshow an incredibly high degree of spread with how this upper \nlow evolves. Expect we will get some more widespread/moderate \nrain out of it at some point but timing is very up in the air.",
